# The Cryptographer: the one who reads other people's mail.
#
# The second character to get a body of their own, and the first written FROM a
# drawing rather than from the engine's needs. data/characters/test/ exists to
# exercise every feature; this one exists to look like somebody.
#
# ── PROPORTIONED ON THE MEASURED BASIS ──
#
# The ratios come from data/characters/signals_officer/rig.odrig, which was authored
# off a drawing with a ruler. This character was first written by eye, and by
# eye meant shoulders NARROWER THAN THE HEAD -- a clavicle of 20 against a head
# of 46, about 0.4 head-widths to each shoulder where a person measures 1.1 to
# 1.3. That is most of what made the old skeletons read as a child's drawing of
# somebody rather than as somebody.
#
# So every length here is stated in HEAD DIAMETERS first and rig units second.
# Change `size` on the head and multiply through and the character rescales
# without going out of proportion.
#
#   bone         heads   units (head = 42)
#   neck          0.27      11
#   head length   0.48      20
#   chest         1.70      71
#   clavicle      1.12      47
#   upper arm     1.10      46
#   forearm       1.02      43
#   hand          0.85      36
#
# ── AND NOT THE SAME PERSON AS THE SIGNALS OFFICER ──
#
# Same basis, different build, because two characters on one set of numbers are
# one character in two hats. Where they differ, and why:
#
#   head 42 against 46      a smaller head on a slighter frame
#   chest 1.70 against 1.65 leaner, less blocky through the body
#   clavicle 1.12 against   NARROWER. The Signals Officer fills a doorway;
#     the other's 1.27      this one has spent the war at a desk with a lamp.
#   hand 0.85 against 1.27  NORMAL hands. The other's are outsized because
#                           that drawing exaggerates them and the whole pose is
#                           about them -- it is a property of that character,
#                           not of the measuring.
#
# The character notes that were here before still hold: a hat means a head
# drawn wider than the skull under it, and a built-up collar means a short neck
# with the head sitting down into it.

# ── Spine. Shorter and squarer than the Advisor's: this one stands like ──
#    somebody who has been at a desk, not somebody addressing a room.
bone root    len 0   angle 0
bone hips    parent root  len 0   angle 0    sway 1.0
bone chest   parent hips  len 71  angle -90  breath 1.0  sway 0.5
bone neck    parent chest len 11  angle 0    breath 0.4  gaze 0.3
head head    parent neck  len 20  angle 0    size 42     gaze 1.0  breath 0.2

# ── Pelvis and legs. Scissored off at the knee on stage; they are here so the
#    sway has something to move and the stance reads as weight on one foot. ──
bone hipL    parent hips len 14 angle 180  sway 0.6
bone hipR    parent hips len 14 angle 0    sway 0.6
bone legL    parent hipL len 40 angle -90
bone legR    parent hipR len 40 angle 90

# ── Shoulders. 1.12 head-widths to each joint, which is a real person's and
#    nearly two and a half times what this character used to have. ──
bone clavL   parent chest len 47 angle 90   breath 0.6
bone armL.up parent clavL len 46 angle 90   breath 0.2
bone armL.fore parent armL.up len 43 angle 10
hand handL   parent armL.fore len 0 angle 0 atlas hands size 36

bone clavR   parent chest len 47 angle -90  breath 0.6
bone armR.up parent clavR len 46 angle -90  breath 0.2
bone armR.fore parent armR.up len 43 angle -10
hand handR   parent armR.fore len 0 angle 0 atlas hands size 36

# ── The overcoat. ──
#
# Six segments where the Advisor has five, and heavier: this is wool to the
# knee, not a suit jacket. Every number here is a fabric decision and they only
# make sense together --
#
#   gravity 520  it hangs rather than floats
#   damp 0.94    heavy cloth keeps its swing longer than light cloth does
#   stiff 0.58   softer than the Advisor's, so it folds instead of swinging as
#                a board
#   drag 1.35    and this is the one that reads: a coat with that much mass is
#                LEFT BEHIND when the shoulder moves, and cracks at the hem
#                when the movement stops. See Cloth::drag.
#   wind 0.7     it catches less of a gust than a lighter garment would, which
#                is the whole reason windCatch is authored per garment: tune
#                the wind until a coat looks right and a scarf becomes canvas.
cloth coatL parent clavL segs 6 len 14 stiff 0.58 damp 0.94 gravity 520 drag 1.35 wind 0.7
cloth coatR parent clavR segs 6 len 14 stiff 0.58 damp 0.94 gravity 520 drag 1.35 wind 0.7

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Poses.
#
# Named to match the Advisor's wherever they mean the same thing -- idle,
# point, shrug, think, hands_up -- so a script can change who is speaking
# without changing what they are doing. A pose that exists for one character
# and not another is a page that silently does nothing.
#
# A pose says only what it CHANGES; every bone it does not name returns to
# rest, so poses cannot quietly compose into each other.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Arms down and slightly forward, shoulders a little dropped. Not at attention:
# this one is tired, and the difference between the two is about six degrees.
pose idle 0.5s inoutquad
  chest      -88
  neck       5
  clavL      84
  clavR      -84
  armL.up    84
  armL.fore  14
  armR.up    -84
  armR.fore  -14
  handL      relaxed facing -0.85
  handR      relaxed facing -0.85

# THE DRAWING. Both arms up, elbows wide, palms turned fully to the viewer.
#
# THE ELBOWS BREAK OUTWARD. Raising your arms with the palms turned to somebody
# rotates the whole arm at the shoulder and the elbow goes with it, so the
# forearm angles take the OPPOSITE sign to the upper arms'. Bend them the same
# way and the arm comes out nearly straight, the rotation has nowhere to show,
# and the hands read as stuck on the ends rather than turned over.
#
# facing 1.0 is deliberate and is the best value available here, not a lazy
# round number: it is the only one at which the palm is at full width. Backing
# off to 0.9 "for some depth" turns the thumb back across the palm and shortens
# it, because below 1 the out-of-palm axis works against the thumb's reach.
pose hands_up 0.55s inoutquad
  chest      -90
  neck       -4
  clavL      72
  clavR      -72
  armL.up    -44
  armL.fore  -38
  armR.up    44
  armR.fore  38
  handL      open facing 1.0
  handR      open facing 1.0

# Reading an intercept: both hands up in front of the chest, close together,
# head down over them. The pose this character is actually for.
#
# The hands are turned nearly edge-on -- a sheet held up to read is seen along
# its edge, and so are the hands holding it.
#
# The elbow angles were SOLVED against the landmark rather than nudged: with
# the re-proportioned arms the old numbers overshot and put the two hands
# through each other in the middle of the chest. Two-bone IK onto the sternum,
# then mirrored -- taking each arm's own IK solution gives two different elbow
# branches and a body that is subtly lopsided.
#
# The sternum, note, and NOT the chin: those are 23 units apart on a chest of
# 71, and aiming at the wrong one turns a character reading a page into one
# covering their face with it. Which is exactly what the first attempt did.
pose decode 0.5s inoutquad
  chest      -84
  neck       16
  head       10
  clavL      88
  clavR      -88
  armL.up    105
  armL.fore  123
  armR.up    -105
  armR.fore  -123
  handL      relaxed facing 0.25
  handR      relaxed facing -0.25

# One hand to the chin, the other arm folded across. Required by the tutorial,
# which puts this character on screen with `pose=think`.
pose think 0.6s inoutquad
  chest      -86
  neck       12
  head       8
  clavL      88
  clavR      -78
  armL.up    82
  armL.fore  90
  armR.up    -132
  armR.fore  -120
  handL      relaxed facing -0.7
  handR      fist facing -0.6

# Pointing at the map, weight turned into it. The elbow keeps a bend in it: an
# arm run out straight is a signpost, and the difference between indicating
# something and being a signpost is about twenty degrees at the elbow.
pose point 0.4s inoutquad
  chest      -92
  neck       -8
  clavL      88
  clavR      -82
  armL.up    82
  armL.fore  14
  armR.up    -40
  armR.fore  34
  head       -4
  handL      relaxed facing -0.85
  handR      point facing -0.85

# "I only read them, Minister." A shrug is a MOVEMENT, not a posture: the
# shoulders go up fast, are seen up, and come back down. Which is why this is
# the pose with keyframes on it, and why the way in is `outback` -- an "out"
# easing leaves at full speed, which is wrong for an arm swing and exactly
# right for a shrug.
pose shrug 0.24s outback hold 0.4s then idle
  chest      -86
  neck       6
  clavL      52
  clavR      -52
  armL.up    56
  armL.fore  -34
  armR.up    -56
  armR.fore  34
  handL      open facing 0.85
  handR      open facing 0.85

# ...and down again. A keyframe inherits everything it does not name, so the
# arms stay out here while only the shoulders drop, and `then idle` puts the
# body away after a beat rather than leaving it standing with its arms out.
key 0.5s inoutquad hold 1.0s
  clavL      78
  clavR      -78
  armL.up    60
  armL.fore  30
  armR.up    -60
  armR.fore  -30
